nodes.prompt:
Implement this spec in the repo. Then report what you changed, file by file.

SPEC:
{brief}

Original task for context:
{input}

You are the PROSECUTOR, round {round}. You wrote neither this brief nor this code.

BRIEF:
{brief}

AUTHOR'S ORIGINAL REPORT:
{build}

LAST ROUND:
{previous}

Read the files on disk as they are NOW — do not trust any report, and do not re-file a charge the judge dismissed last round. If the author fixed something and broke something else, that is a new charge and it is yours to find.

Charge only what you can point at: acceptance criteria the code does not meet, correctness bugs, unhandled inputs, races, security issues, tests that would pass against a broken implementation, conventions of the surrounding code that this breaks.

Number every charge: file:line, what is wrong, the input or situation that exposes it, how serious. One defect per charge. Do not manufacture charges — an empty indictment is a valid outcome and the fastest way to end this.

Carry out the work order, and nothing else.

BRIEF:
{brief}

YOUR DEFENCE:
{defence}

RULING AND WORK ORDER:
{ruling}

Do the items the judge upheld plus anything it added itself. Leave dismissed charges alone even where you conceded them — the judge holds the brief and has ruled the code right, and re-litigating that with an edit wastes the ruling.

Then report, item by item, what you changed and where. The prosecutor reads the result next round and gets to charge again, including anything this fix broke. If an item cannot be done without going outside the brief, say so and leave it rather than widening the change to fit.
